abstract: >-
  Scripts, certificates, run logs and Lean 4 verifications supporting the paper
  "The Sphere Packing Problem in Dimension 4 and the Twenty-Four-Cell
  Conjecture": the exact chamber certificates of the single-deviation bound, the
  covering bound, the root-system and 600-cell enumerations, the slack
  continuation, and the three-point semidefinite certificate for the twenty-
  three-contact case with its verification in exact rational, interval and Lean
  arithmetic. It also carries the two steps of the certificate of de Laat,
  Leijenhorst and de Muinck Keizer for the twenty-four-contact case that their
  own code leaves out, the construction of the zonal matrices and the four
  polynomial identities, recomputed from their published data in exact rational
  arithmetic on an ordinary machine; the distance criterion for neighbours that
  do not touch the centre and a numerical search of the case it leaves open; the
  verification of the local bound whenever at most twenty-three centres lie
  within sqrt 6 (labelled_certificate_check.py); the exact content of the
  further reductions, also in Lean 4; the explicit constant epsilon_0 = 4e-26 of
  the near-contact theorem (explicit_eps0.py); the three-point certificate on an
  enlarged domain that makes the kissing number in dimension four stable, no 25
  points of S^3 with inner products at most 1/2 + 0.008
  (certify_cardinality.py); and an independent re-verification of every
  computation of the paper. The directory level2 writes the zonal matrices in
  the file format of the code of de Laat, Leijenhorst and de Muinck Keizer, so
  that their second-level programme runs on an ordinary machine, and checks
  them against that code entry by entry; count_survey.py minimises the
  truncated volume for every count of centres within sqrt 6. Version v1.9.0
  adds the second order of the cell volume at the root system, an exact closed
  curve of contact cells of volume 8, a second twenty-four-point code far from
  the root system, the reduction of the conjecture to two explicit
  statements, a proof of the second of them for thirty-one or more centres
  and its reduction at thirty to a single configuration, and a proof of the
  first along the push-outs of the root system; fifteen parts of the paper
  are checked in Lean 4. Version v1.10.0 shows that the second level with a
  margin on pairs cannot localise twenty-four points at slack 0.008 at any
  degree, proves that with 25 to 33 centres and T <= 8 twenty-four lie within
  2.444, bounds how many of the twenty-four centres of (G) can lie far out,
  and gives the two-point bound for every count from 25 to 49. Version
  v1.11.0 bounds, for 25 to 30 centres, how many lie within 2.05, 2.1, 2.15
  and 2.2 and within which radius twenty-four of them lie, and splits the
  case left open at thirty into two shells. Version v1.11.1 adds two such
  counts and shows in floating point that the labelled three-point programme
  cannot exclude a further centre beside twenty-four close ones at degrees 10
  and 12. The re-verification and these
  programs were developed, and their computations run, with the assistance of
  Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic.
